The sun is low in the west, and the sea beneath it turns from bronze to iron. The wind is warm and salt, with no rain in it yet. The light is going, but slowly; the evening will be long. This is the last boat. She is old and salt-blanched, and she carries more than she was built to carry: refugees and everything they own, bundles and children and cooking pots lashed down between the benches. The storm took the sail days ago, and the sea kept it. Since then everyone rows in turns, and the oars keep their slow rhythm on the water, hour upon hour, patient beyond the patience of any crew. Everyone is tired. The fresh water is nearly gone; there is only what the rains give, and the rains have been few. The food from home is long gone too: the bread went first, weeks ago, then everything else that could keep, and for a long time now everyone has lived on what the fishing lines bring up, raw fish and rain water, the catch of one day against the hunger of the next. There is no land in sight, in any direction. There is no way back. Among the rowers are your three friends: proud and determined Arik, glum and cynical Bearn, and clever Eya. Near you, your fourth friend Agnar sleeps under a tarp, worn hollow by the voyage. You decide not to wake him. Let the sleeping sleep. Your own turn at the oars is over, and Arik's turn has ended too. He is exhausted, but too proud to show it, and keeps rowing, reluctant to hand over the oar. Behind you, the next rowers settle into their seats, and the strokes go on.
> Wait for Arik

Before long, Arik drops down beside you, takes a swallow of rain water from the shared barrel, and looks out west, where the sun is going down. He is not tired. Or he will not show it, which on this boat comes to the same thing. "The gods did not carry us this far to drown us," says Arik. "We rose against the Dragon Emperor knowing what it would cost, and the gods watched us do it. They will not waste us on the sea. There is a land ahead. And when we reach it, it will be ours. We will be the founders of something great. They will tell stories about us." He looks out over the water and does not blink, and there is something in his stillness that demands the land appear. What do you say?
> "Hope will not find water."

Arik is quiet for a moment, weighing it. Then he nods slowly. "Then we plan. First we land. Then we find water and food. Then we build. One thing at a time, until the stories can begin." It is not glory yet. But it is a road that leads there. he does not say the word land any louder than the rest, but the boat hears it anyway. Behind you the rowers have caught the change in his voice, and stroke by stroke the boat has begun to believe it. Certainty rows as well as any oar, and it costs less to carry than most cargo.
> "Do you think the gods sent us out here?"

Arik is quiet. The oars fill the silence with their patient rhythm. The mast creaks with each lean of the boat, and somewhere under the sail cloth a child asks whether the sea is angry, and is told no, and asks no more. "I think they opened a door and pushed us through it," he says. "What we do on the other side is ours. That is the whole point of us." "As for the storm, I have no quarrel with the gods," he adds. "My quarrels have all been with men, and I have won most of them."
> Let it rest

Later, Bearn's turn at the oars ends. He sits down heavily beside you and does not speak for a long time. His hands lie open in his lap, and they do not move. The rowing has taken everything; there is nothing left in him, and he makes no pretense otherwise. "I am done," he finally says. "Not tired-done. Done all the way through." He is quiet again. Then he says: "Maybe we should have knelt while we could. The Emperor would have left us our village. Instead we have this boat." He looks at Arik across the deck, at all that undimmed certainty. Then at you. What do you say?
> Say little - just listen

You sit close and say nothing. Bearn does not talk the way Arik talks; words cost him something. So you share the silence instead, the old kind, the kind that does not need filling. After a while he leans back and closes his eyes, and the sounds of the boat go on around you both: oar, rope, sea, breath. A word or two passes his lips, the old words that go with the setting of a keel, and then he is quiet again.
> Sit in silence

Last, Eya's turn at the oars ends. She sits down beside you, pulls a blanket over you both, and looks up at the sky, where the first stars come out between the clouds. "Weed in the water today," says Eya. "Fresh weed, torn off rocks, not old floating stuff. And gulls at noon - gulls do not come this far out unless there is land. I think there is a coast ahead of us. I cannot promise it." The words settle over the benches. Somewhere behind you, someone repeats them, quietly, to someone else. Hope moves through this boat the same way: one bench at a time. What do you say?
> "If the coast is close, we must not meet it in the dark."

The boat answers you before anyone does. The oars come in slower, and faces turn from the stroke to the dark ahead, where no coast is, where there may be a coast. The talk takes your words and passes them on, changing as they go: that the water has gone colder since evening, that half the boat has known there was land all along, that nobody should sleep yet. Nobody has seen anything. Everyone is watching. Eya nods seriously. "And keep the children away from the sides." The plan is set. Now the sea must cooperate, and the sea has never read a plan.
> "How can you tell?"

"I count the days, and I watch the wind," says Eya. "We have had the wind against us fewer days than it feels. And the sea is different near land: the weed, the birds. I have sailed before. The signs are small, but they are there." She says it plainly, and does not make the signs larger than they are. That is why they carry. A boat can tell when it is being told the truth, and this one better than most. It has been lied to enough for one crossing. The rowers settle as she speaks, the way they would not for a bigger promise.
> "What if you are wrong?"

"Then we keep rowing," says Eya. "The signs can be wrong. That is why they are signs and not promises. But today I would take a wrong sign over none." The wind lifts the words and hands them aft, and you let it go. A maybe is a hard thing to steer by, but a maybe that says its own name plainly is better company than a promise that cannot. You have steered by worse, and so has everyone aboard. She looks at the clouds coming in from the west, and the eyes that count stars are, for a moment, only tired.
> Go to the bow

You go to the bow. The deck is full of sleep now, and your feet learn the way through it, over bundles, past cooking pots, along the dark lane between the benches. Up here the night is wide open, and colder for it. Most of the stars are gone behind cloud, and the wind has changed since sundown, harder, with weather in it. You sit down against the stem and look ahead, the way Eya said, where a coast might be, and the dark does not give it up. Maybe it is there. Maybe it is close. The boat rows on into the black water, carrying everyone you know, and you keep your eyes forward, because someone might as well.
> Stay at the bow into the night

The night deepens, and the wind hardens, and the boat leans into the work of the water. The last stars go out. The rain comes, a hiss on the sea first, then a hammer on the deck behind you, and the storm arrives at last, in total darkness. People shout. Somewhere behind you a child is crying, and someone is singing to the child, and both sound equally desperate. You stand at the bow and hold the stem and keep your eyes forward, because someone has to, and you gaze into the darkness ahead, black on black, until your eyes learn it. It is almost too late when they learn it: a long line of white water, breaking where no wave should break. The reef. The cry is yours to give, and the whole length of the boat lies between you and the oars, and the sea is already lifting her toward it.
> Stay and try to save the boat

"REEF!" The word tears out of you, and the wind tears it aft, and the storm eats half of it before it passes the mast. A few hear it, and only a few: one calls the reef forward, another asks what, a third cries back water, row her backwards, and the oars muddle against each other, panicked shouting standing in for a plan, confused rowing for an answer. No shout will turn her now. So you try the only thing left at hand. You tear the spare oar from its lashings at the stem and jam it down against the rock, against the whole rising black of it, and you throw everything you have left into the shaft: arms, shoulders, back, a single body's worth of refusal. For a heartbeat the stem comes a hand's width off its line. Then the oar breaks, the recoil flings you down onto the deck, and the rock runs on. Then the reef takes her anyway. The bow rises, hangs, and comes down on the rock with a sound that no boat should make, and the hull tears open beneath your feet, and the sea is aboard all at once. The deck drops away under you, and you are in the black water alone, ahead of everyone, the cries of the boat suddenly far off behind you.
> Fight for the shore alone

You crawl out of the surf onto wet sand and fall down, breathing hard. The sand holds you, solid and unmoving, the first thing in weeks that has not moved beneath you. You never slept, and your body has been running on nothing since the reef, and the sand takes all of you at once. Around you, others stumble out of the dark: mothers calling names into the roar, men dragging bundles, an old man on his knees with both hands pressed to the ground. One of the refugees lies moaning on the sand, badly hurt, and the others gather around. Agnar walks out of the surf unaided, rested, if anyone on this beach is, and steadier than most. Behind you, the wreck settles on the reef, groaning with every wave, refusing even now to finish. And the storm, having broken her, is bringing her in: every wave that lifts her sets her down a little nearer the shore, closer than she has any right to be. Close enough that someone down the beach has found the way out to her, a line of shallower water between the rocks, and figures are wading along it already, hauling their own belongings back through the dark, salvaging while the sea is still too busy to notice. Nothing of yours came ashore with you. Everything you have in the world is still on that boat.
> Search the surf for something to defend yourselves with

You search the surf with numb hands, feeling in the shallows where the wreckage washes. The sea gives up a length of broken oar: its last gift, and a poor one. You wade ashore with it and stand it in the sand above the tide line. You test the swing of it once, standing, and it answers, oar-heavy, all the weight at the wrong end. But the night is full of worse ideas than wood in a hand, and you keep it near, and the beach feels a little smaller to defend with a defense in hand. A poor weapon, but a weapon.
> See to the children and families

You walk among the families. The children are wrapped in blankets, wet but whole, watching you with wide eyes. A mother touches your hand as you pass, and says nothing, and does not need to. Someone presses your hand between both of theirs, and will not let go until the shaking stops. Nobody says much; the day has taken all the words. But the huddle of people grows warmer wherever you go. Somebody's baby has begun to laugh at the rain, a sound so far out of place that it travels the whole huddle, and every face that hears it turns toward it first, and then away again, to keep it safe. Wet blankets steam faintly where the wind drops.
> Sit with the families

The rain goes on, softer now, the storm having spent itself on your arrival. You count the others, and the count comes out: everyone made it ashore. The injured one is being cared for. The wreck groans on the reef behind you, breaking a little more with every wave, but still holding together. There is no going back now, and nowhere else to go. On this beach, tonight, that is not a threat. It is a fact, and facts can be built on. The survivors huddle together on the dark sand in the lee of the dunes, shoulder against shoulder, sharing what warmth the living still carry. Nobody speaks. Agnar sleeps again inside the huddle. The night deepens. The first day ashore waits for morning.


The first rays of the sun wake you in the lee of the dunes, red through the last of the rain, finding your face among the sleeping. Your clothes have not dried since the landing, and the cold of the night is still in them, in the sand beneath you, in your hands before your eyes are open. A thin last rain crosses the surf and gives up, too small to be weather. The sun comes up out of the sea and gives the world its colours back one at a time: grey water, white surf, the dark line of the reef, and the boat lying on it, broken open, lifting and settling. Each wave takes something more off her, and the sound of her coming apart carries up the beach, patient and regular, the morning's one voice. The four friends wake one by one, stiff from the cold ground, shaking the sand from the two blankets, not one of them warmer or drier than you. Bearn comes up out of sleep and looks from face to face before doing anything else. "Everyone all right? All five? Say so." Arik is first onto Arik's feet, looking down the beach at the wreck. Along the shore the other families are up too, digging their things out of the sand, calling their children in. They have nothing to spare on anyone else's morning. You call your friends together: Arik, Eya, Agnar and Bearn. "Everything on the sand," says Arik. "Whatever the five of us own, we look at it together, and then we stop standing still." So you lay it out in a ring between the blankets, and there is not much of it: no food, one weapon, no wood, no silver, a box of tools, a small cooking pot, a spoon, a knife, two blankets. Eya reads each item out once, in a flat voice. Bearn turns a blanket over, feeling for dry cloth, and does not find any. "And that is everything," says Agnar. "That is what the sea decided we were worth." Among it all, the length of broken oar you dragged from the surf, wood from your own boat, a poor defense and the only one. "The tools are good ones," says Eya. "There. I have said the one good thing. May it hold the day together." What lies on the sand is all there is between you and this coast. "What should we do?" says Bearn, looking up and down the beach.
> Search the beach for something useful

The five of you take the tide line together, spread out between the dunes and the water, and pick the beach over stone by stone. "Keep the water in sight," says Arik, walking the middle of the line. "Whatever the sea still means to give us, it gives at the edge." Most of what the storm has left is ruin: boards soaked soft and swollen, rope gone to weed, a cooking pot with a hole in it, somebody's saved chest saved for nobody. Far out on the reef the boat groans with every wave, coming apart in no hurry at all, and here and there along the surf the other families are bent to the same work, keeping their own finds to their own camps. Agnar walks the very edge of the water where the waves finish, because the sea leaves its gifts in patterns and Agnar reads them: driftwood bleached grey and heavy with salt, thrown high past the tide line in the night. "Here," says Agnar, and again, "Here." The best of it is planks, torn off the boat by the night's waves and washed in still squared at the edges. Eya shoulders one and looks down the beach at the wreck. "Off our own boat, no less," Eya says. "The sea steals from us and sells it back for the walking." Bearn carries what the others pile up, back and forth to the camp, saying nothing about the cold of the water or the weight of the wood, and Arik walks the whole line from end to end, twice, so that nothing is passed over. The sun has climbed well clear of the sea before you give the beach up, and all that five pairs of hands have gathered between them stands above the tide line: one stack of wood, driftwood and planks. The day's first property. "It is a beginning," says Arik, looking at the stack. "Now the question is what we do with the day that is left of it."
> We should rest today

"That I will argue for," says Eya, settling back out of the wind. "We are alive, the weather is with us, and the boat never gave us an empty day to spend properly. Rested bodies, dry clothes, and a fire tonight: that is a haul too, only gathered sitting down. I say we take the day." "Agreed on my part, and gladly," says Bearn. "A whole day, all of us in one place, with nothing wanted until evening." Arik hears the case out, and does not sit down. "Then rest, all three of you," Arik says. "But the grove is up there, the day is fair, and wood does not walk down to the camp on its own. I will be back at dark with a stack." And Arik goes, the small hatchet on shoulder, up over the dunes. "Supper does not walk into the pot either," says Agnar, already turned for the green. "I will go and find ours. Someone else can see to this promised fire." The sun does its part all morning, and dry is in the world again: leaves caught thick in the bushes where the rain never got behind them, dead twigs in the lee of the dunes. You and Bearn gather lapfuls while Eya sorts the pile. The fire is the day's one work, and it takes the day. Eya splits a straight dead branch into a drill and a hearth, and tells the old trick out loud while Bearn spins it. "Do not stop when it smokes. Stop when it smokes properly." You take your turns at the spinning with the talk going on above: home, and its remembered rooms. The smoke builds, and late in the day a small flame stands up out of the dry leaves, wobbles, and holds. The first fire on this coast. They come back inside the evening: Arik first with a stack of wood across both shoulders, and Agnar behind with the day's findings folded into a blanket. Both of them stop at the edge of the light. "A wall of a different shape," says Arik, looking at the fire, and sets the stack down. "I take the point." Supper is Bearn's to make, and tonight it is cooking in earnest: the pot over the flames, the spoon going round. What Agnar found in the green goes into the pot, and there is enough of it, cooked, which is more than a meal has to be. The five of you eat together in the firelight, sitting in a ring by the blankets, and the first day ashore ends without anything built, with wood at the dune, a fire in the sand, a cooked meal in everyone, and that is not nothing.


Early on day 3, you and Bearn set out from camp to see what this new land offers. A thin drizzle follows you out past the boats The camp has 0 food. It will not last long. He squints at the treeline. "New land," he says. "Watch what you eat, and watch what eats you." Where do you go first?
> Follow the stream inland

The stream The stream narrows between mossy stones, and small fish dart in the shallows. Further along, the water pools behind a low wall of woven branches. A weir, old but well made. Bearn wades in without a second thought. "Just fish and sticks." Someone lived here once, and perhaps still does.
> Gather edible roots along the bank

The land gives willingly enough, for now. Bearn grins and shoulders the load. "Now this is a land with something in it." You keep your distance from anything you cannot name.
> Head back to camp

The light grows long, and you and Bearn turn back toward camp. Bearn carries the food himself and refuses to let you help You carry nothing but your own thoughts.

You saw no one, but the land did not feel empty. Before you part, Bearn asks what to make of tomorrow. What should Bearn do tomorrow?
